__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"COLLECTIONS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"wordCount",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"fullText",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const t=[{id:'mars',topic:'Getting to Mars',emoji:'\ud83d\ude80',blurb:'What it takes to reach the red planet, and to fly there.',texts:[{id:'mars-1',title:'Seven Months to Mars',paras:['Mars is about half as wide as Earth, but getting there is a long trip. Even when the two planets line up well, a spacecraft takes about seven months to arrive.','Mars would not be an easy place to live. Its average temperature is about 80 degrees below zero Fahrenheit. Its atmosphere is so thin that it has less than 1 percent of the air that Earth has, and almost all of it is carbon dioxide. Gravity is weaker too: you would weigh about one third of what you weigh at home.','Even talking to home would be slow. A message takes several minutes to travel between the planets, so a quick back-and-forth conversation is impossible.'],gloss:[{w:'atmosphere',means:'The layer of gases around a planet.'},{w:'gravity',means:'The pull that keeps things on the ground.'},{w:'average',means:'The usual or middle amount when you look at many numbers.'}],ask:'Which problem would be hardest for people living on Mars, and why?',source:'Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History Mars fact sheet (2021); NASA Mars facts; Space.com.'},{id:'mars-2',title:'A Helicopter on Another Planet',paras:['On February 18, 2021, NASA\u2019s Perseverance rover landed in a place on Mars called Jezero Crater. It is about the size of a car, and it carries cameras, microphones and science tools to study rocks and look for signs of ancient life.','Tucked under the rover was something new: a small helicopter named Ingenuity. Flying on Mars is hard because the air is so thin that there is little for the blades to push against.','On April 19, 2021, Ingenuity lifted off, hovered and landed. It was the first powered, controlled flight on another planet. In 1903, the Wright brothers made the first powered flight on Earth. Now, 118 years later, engineers had done it on another world.'],gloss:[{w:'rover',means:'A robot vehicle that drives around to explore.'},{w:'ancient',means:'From a very, very long time ago.'},{w:'engineers',means:'People who design and build machines and structures.'}],ask:'Why was it harder to fly on Mars than on Earth? Use something from the first text in your answer.',source:'NASA Science: \u201cIngenuity Mars Helicopter Successfully Completes First Flight\u201d (Apr 19, 2021); NASA Perseverance landing (Feb 18, 2021); Wright brothers first flight Dec 17, 1903 (Discovery).'}]},{id:'ocean',topic:'The deep ocean',emoji:'\ud83c\udf0a',blurb:'Darkness, crushing pressure, and animals that make their own light.',texts:[{id:'ocean-1',title:'Into the Dark',paras:['Sunlight fades fast in the ocean. Below about 200 meters, or roughly 650 feet, almost no sunlight reaches. Everything deeper lives in darkness.','The deepest known spot is the Challenger Deep in the Pacific Ocean\u2019s Mariana Trench, almost 11,000 meters down. That is deeper than Mount Everest is tall.','The water above pushes down with enormous force. Pressure rises by about one atmosphere for every 10 meters you go down. At the bottom of the Challenger Deep, it is more than 1,000 times the pressure at the surface. Only a few specially built vehicles have ever carried people there.'],gloss:[{w:'pressure',means:'How hard something is pushing on something else.'},{w:'trench',means:'A long, deep, narrow ditch. In the ocean, a very deep valley in the sea floor.'},{w:'enormous',means:'Very, very big.'}],ask:'What would a vehicle need to survive a trip to the Challenger Deep?',source:'NOAA and oceanographic data (Challenger Deep ~10,935 m, Greenaway et al. 2021); deep-sea fact sheets on pressure (about 1 atmosphere per 10 m).'},{id:'ocean-2',title:'Living Lights',paras:['If there is no sunlight in the deep, how do animals see? Many of them make their own light. This is called bioluminescence.','In 2017, scientists studied a huge library of underwater video and found that about three quarters of the ocean animals they observed could glow, or very likely could. It is not just the famous anglerfish. Jellies, worms and squid glow too.','Animals use light in clever ways: to find a mate, to signal to others of their kind, and to work together in groups. Scientists are still learning what much of this light means.'],gloss:[{w:'bioluminescence',means:'Light made by a living thing.'},{w:'observed',means:'Watched carefully and noticed.'},{w:'signal',means:'To send a message without words, like a flash or a wave.'}],ask:'From the first text, why would making light be so useful below 200 meters?',source:'Martini & Haddock, Scientific Reports (2017), via Live Science and Practical Fishkeeping (MBARI video database); deep-sea bioluminescence uses, Learning Mole.'}]},{id:'paycheck',topic:'Your first paycheck',emoji:'\ud83d\udcb5',blurb:'Why the check is smaller than you expected, and what to do with it.',texts:[{id:'pay-1',title:'Where Did My Money Go?',paras:['Say you earn $500 in your first two weeks at a job. Then your paycheck shows less than that. What happened?','Your gross pay is everything you earned. Your net pay, or take-home pay, is what is left after money is withheld. Part of what is withheld goes to Social Security and Medicare. Together they take 7.65 percent: 6.2 percent for Social Security and 1.45 percent for Medicare. On $500, that is about $38. Your employer pays the same amount again on top.','There may also be income tax. When you start a job, you fill out a form called a W-4. It tells your employer how much federal income tax to hold back from each check.'],gloss:[{w:'gross',means:'The total amount before anything is taken out.'},{w:'withheld',means:'Held back and not paid to you; sent to the government instead.'},{w:'employer',means:'The person or company you work for.'}],ask:'If you earned $1,000, about how much would go to Social Security and Medicare?',source:'IRS FICA rates for 2026 (6.2% Social Security, 1.45% Medicare), via Paychex, NerdWallet and Xero guides; IRS Form W-4.'},{id:'pay-2',title:'A Plan for the Rest',paras:['Once the money is in your account, it is easy for it to disappear. A budget is simply a plan for where it will go before you spend it.','One simple rule of thumb splits take-home pay three ways: about half for needs, like rides to work and your phone bill; some for wants, like food out or games; and a set amount for savings. The exact split matters less than deciding ahead of time.','Saving a little from every paycheck adds up. Saving $25 from each check, every two weeks, is $650 in a year. That could become an emergency fund, so a surprise bill doesn\u2019t knock you off track.'],gloss:[{w:'budget',means:'A plan for how you will spend and save money.'},{w:'emergency',means:'A sudden, serious problem you didn\u2019t plan for.'},{w:'savings',means:'Money you keep instead of spending.'}],ask:'Using your net pay from the first text, how would you split it? What would you save for?',source:'Arithmetic: $25 \xd7 26 two-week paychecks = $650.'}]},{id:'train',topic:'Train like a pro',emoji:'\ud83c\udfc0',blurb:'What science says about sleep, practice and getting better.',texts:[{id:'train-1',title:'The Sleep Experiment',paras:['At Stanford University, researcher Cheri Mah asked 11 players on the men\u2019s basketball team to try something unusual: spend at least 10 hours in bed every night for several weeks.','The players ended up sleeping almost two hours more per night than before. Their sprint times got faster, from 16.2 seconds to 15.5 seconds. Their free throws and three-pointers both improved by about 9 percent. They also reacted faster and said they felt less tired and in a better mood.','It was a small study of one team, so it can\u2019t prove that more sleep helps everyone the same way. But it suggests that many athletes are short on sleep and that rest may be part of training, not a break from it.'],gloss:[{w:'researcher',means:'A person who studies something carefully to find out what\u2019s true.'},{w:'improved',means:'Got better.'},{w:'suggests',means:'Points toward something without proving it.'}],ask:'Why can\u2019t one small study prove that sleep helps every athlete?',source:'Mah, Mah, Kezirian & Dement, \u201cThe effects of sleep extension on the athletic performance of collegiate basketball players,\u201d SLEEP 34(7), 2011.'},{id:'train-2',title:'Is Practice Everything?',paras:['You may have heard that practice is the secret to becoming great. Some researchers argued that the amount of focused, effortful practice explains most of the difference between stars and everyone else.','In 2014, a team of psychologists combined the results of 88 studies to test that idea. Practice mattered, a lot. But in sports, it explained about 18 percent of the differences in performance. In games like chess, about 26 percent. In music, about 21 percent.','So what else matters? Probably many things: when you started, coaching, health, sleep, and more. The takeaway is not that practice doesn\u2019t work. It does. It\u2019s that practice is one important piece, not the whole puzzle.'],gloss:[{w:'psychologists',means:'Scientists who study how people think, feel and act.'},{w:'performance',means:'How well someone does something.'},{w:'focused',means:'Paying close attention to one thing.'}],ask:'Name two things besides practice that could help a player improve. Use one from the first text.',source:'Macnamara, Hambrick & Oswald, \u201cDeliberate practice and performance in music, games, sports, education, and professions: a meta-analysis,\u201d Psychological Science 25(8), 2014.'}]}],o=t=>t.paras.join(' ').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length,s=t=>`${t.title}. ${t.paras.join(' ')}`},950,[]);